{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1556899119,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIxNzgxOTk4Mw=="},"target":"6f870072757f6e2e319928258f226be5bed900dd063cd056bb4462abecbf07b5","message":"There is a dilemma between the desire to have a compact API and some customizability of command behavior. Example: `rev-create` does not have  `--text-no-annex`, but it can come handy to be able to get such a setup conveniently.\n\nI believe a \"hook\" mechanism is one way to have this. Here is an example how a command invocation could look like that could be supported to run a dedicated configuration procedure\nafter a dataset got created:\n\n  `datalad -c datalad.hooks.create-done=cfg_text2git rev-create`\n\nThis uses our tooling to have a temporary config setting, but of course such a setting could also become part of a dataset/user/system configuration.\n\nUsing this mechanism would be nice, because we already have a need to improve config override handling for better control of Git/Git-annex behavior within low-level commands (#3246, and various failed attempts along the lines of #3204, #2851, #2818, #905). There we face the exact same issue: desire to fine-tune, without the willingness or ability to explode the API to be able to pass through tons of special-purpose options.\n\nTODOs if we want to move forward with this:\n\n- agree on a nomenclature of such config keys. Proposal: as above, with last key segment being `command`-`custom slot name` (where each command describes its slots in its docs)\n- agree on a syntax of such config values (plain procedure names, or stringified Python list of  arguments for run-procedure, or ...\n\nATM we do have `--proc-pre`, `--proc-post` arguments at the cmdline API level, but those do not scale and are hard to use for a specific customization within a larger command. I think they should be removed.\n\nEventually, we should have the ability to define criteria to detect specific results that pass through our main \"event-loop\", and to trigger specific actions/hooks. However, until we have that, I see no problem in implementing the execution of an initial set of hooks (like the example above) within individual command implementations. Importantly, those should merely define the position of a hook within the processing flow of a command, but not the nature of an intervention. So in the above example we simply have a loop over `ds.config.get('datalad.hooks.create-done')` where `run-procedure` is called for each value.\n\nPossible use-cases to inform development\n- populate git-annex metadata for added files (e.g. annotate with `distribution-restrictions` key=value pairs).  ATM [there is no way AFAIK](http://git-annex.branchable.com/git-annex-metadata/#comment-fde59930f108af0fff842f5e25351e93) to do so via `.gitattributes`","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1553335496,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIyMjQyNzU3Njc="},"added":["feedback-desired"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553346468,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg2ODM3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475868370"},"message":"If we consider `create` for now since it is a immediate use-case in question. Where would you store such configuration setting - globally? in the super dataset where you are creating it? Neither of those locations really relates to the fact that I want **new** dataset having this property, and possibly not any other within this dataset.  In some cases though I see them probably being useful - e.g. when I know that the entire hierarchy of datasets should have consistent \"settings\" across.  It would relate not only to `--text-no-annex` but possibly to annex backend, annex init options, etc.  But because we have no configuration inheritance from the super-datasets, I do not see how this would help beyond one level.\n\nAs for `--text-no-annex` specifically it really boils down to be able to specify for a given invocation of `create` and relate it to the created dataset (not its super etc).   Unfortunately even `--proc-post` is not sufficient since newly created dataset is not what it operates on (can't find that reported issue issue - if it was reported - but I remember discussing it), so you have to use `-d newdataset` to be able to run a `--proc-post` procedure on it.  It just becomes a mouthful of a cmdline specification to achieve what I find using frequently, and which thus should have a concise command line specification.  Internally, sure thing, it better uses the procedure to not reimplement the same action.  But at the user level - common modes of operation should be clearly presented in `datalad operation --help` (procedures aren't listed) and have concise syntax to trigger them. procedures/plugins/hooks/... make things more flexible and versatile, but also more obfuscated to the user.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1553347717,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg2OTgyNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475869826"},"message":"For this use case I would not store it at all, but use exactly the command that I gave above.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553348318,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3MDQ3NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475870474"},"message":"yes, so have a mouthfull of `datalad.hooks.create-done=cfg_text2git` for what should IMHO be a short option.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553348318,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIwMDIxMDg4NQ=="},"target":"5c913ff90b22d06df35e6db17ad9935ac273d9f0b0da932a1a3f3bdd22cdfb32","message":"yes, so have a mouthfull of `-c datalad.hooks.create-done=cfg_text2git` for what should IMHO be a short option.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553348605,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3MDc5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475870798"},"message":"Ideally that short option should be homogeneous with the procedure it runs... since you went for a new name `cfg_text2git`, we no longer have harmony there.  I am ok if it would become `--cfg-text2git` option.  Which gives me a crazy idea -- may be (all? or appropriately annotated as applicable) procedures could be exposed as cmdline options to the command(s)? listed in a separate subsection in `command --help`.\nthe only immediate problem I see - order might matter and iirc we do not have ordering information","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1553349425,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3MTg5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475871890"},"message":"Let's keep in mind that this issue is about hooks in general and not about a specific option of create.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1553349850,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3MjQxMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475872412"},"message":"\u003e  Where would you store such configuration setting - globally? in the super dataset where you are creating it?\n\nWhat's wrong with  `~/.gitconfig`?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553351595,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3NDY3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475874670"},"message":"\u003e Let's keep in mind that this issue is about hooks in general and not about a specific option of create.\n\nIt would then be useful to provide other than `create` + `cfg_text2git` example in the description, otherwise it is hard to envision what hooks and when and where I might like to run.\n\n\u003e What's wrong with `~/.gitconfig`?\n\nHardly applicable to the only example in question ATM - I do not want all my datasets to have text2git setup.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1553353287,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3Njc3MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475876771"},"message":"\u003e Hardly applicable to the only example in question ATM - I do not want all my datasets to have text2git setup.\n\nOk. Probably misinterpreted your desire to store it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1553353872,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTg3NzUxMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3264#issuecomment-475877511"},"message":"Generally, I like the idea of such hooks. I'm just wondering whether we could design it more generally. Referencing procedures is something obvious that should be possible and configuring it is nice as well. But may be we can come up with something that melts this idea with the hook concept git uses. Just a thought - need to think that through.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1553372507,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTkwMTU2OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3265#issuecomment-475901569"},"message":"\u003e - ideally, each interim commit should record in its message that it is a part of the \"bigger run\".\n\nHow would that work?  We don't have control over what the command is committing.\n\n\u003e - at the end of the run, if current commit is not the original commit, placing a field in the `run` record, like `parent` containing this commit\n\u003e [...]\n\u003e Then we get only the issue for humans to easily identify that a commit was a part of the larger `run`, which I would find highly desired. (I do look at them and find useful)\n\n\nI haven't given it too much thought, but an alternative would be to use the branch structure to indicate multi-commit runs.  Glossing over some HEAD management, this could be something like\n\n  * Record initial commit.\n  * Run the command and notice that we're no longer on the initial commit.\n  * Save run results as usual.\n  * Merge temporary run branch.\n\n```\n$ datalad run -m'a committing command' \\\n  'git commit --allow-empty -mmsg \u0026\u0026 touch foo'\n\nM   [DATALAD RUNCMD] a committing command\n|\\\n| R [DATALAD RUNCMD] a committing command\n| o msg\n|/\no \u003cstarting commit\u003e\n```\n\nCommit R would be created because this particular command leaves uncommitted changes.  M and R could both have json records, but M should always because R may not exist.\n\nSuch a structure would make it easy for both human and machine inspectors to identify multi-commit runs.\n\n[ Relates to #3096. ]","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1553436815,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NTk2MzM2Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3265#issuecomment-475963366"},"message":"I like branch/merge idea -- probably it is the way to go!  Not sure though if leftover changes should be committed with the same record/message. Could cause confusion.  I would have committed R as \"a committing command [unsaved changes]\" and then have M with the run record\n\n\u003e \u003e * ideally, each interim commit should record in its message that it is a part of the \"bigger run\".\n\u003e \n\u003e How would that work? We don't have control over what the command is committing.\n\n- `run` defines DATALAD_RUN_RECORD env variable\n- `GitRepo.commit` (or at higher `save` level?) appends it to the commit message if variable is defined.\n\nMight relate also to #3236 (automated commit messages). May be such a variable would be `DATALAD_COMMIT_INTENT_MESSAGE` or alike, so top level command defines it to be used in underlying commits (including for git-annex itself).","files":null}]}